  "enrichment": {
    "fodhelper_role": "fodhelper.exe is a signed Microsoft binary (Features On Demand helper) marked autoElevate=true in its manifest. Windows elevates it to high integrity without displaying a UAC consent prompt when the user is a member of the local Administrators group.",
    "hijack_mechanism": "On launch, fodhelper.exe opens the ms-settings: protocol. The shell resolves that protocol's handler through HKCU\\Software\\Classes first, which is writable by the unprivileged user. Writing Shell\\Open\\command there, and setting DelegateExecute to an empty value so the shell falls back to the (Default) verb string, causes fodhelper to launch the attacker's command instead of the settings UI \u2014 at fodhelper's elevated integrity level.",
    "registry_chain": "Four key creations then two value writes, all by powershell.exe PID 1376, build the handler in order: ms-settings, \\Shell, \\Shell\\Open, \\Shell\\Open\\command, then DelegateExecute=(Empty) and (Default)=<command>.",
    "payload_command": "The (Default) value is set to: powershell.exe -NoP -NonI -W Hidden -c $x=$((gp HKCU:Software\\Microsoft\\Windows Update).Update); powershell -NoP -NonI -W Hidden -enc $x \u2014 it reads a base64 blob out of a registry value named 'Update' under a key masquerading as Windows Update, then executes it with -enc.",
    "execution_proof": "powershell.exe PID 7820 is created with fodhelper.exe as its parent and a command line byte-identical to the (Default) value written 0.95 seconds earlier. Parent-child lineage, not just timing, links the registry write to the elevated execution.",
    "no_cleanup_note": "The registry keys are not removed within the captured window. The handler remains in place, so the same elevation can be replayed.",
    "preceding_recon": "whoami.exe /groups executed shortly before the registry writes \u2014 consistent with an operator checking group membership and integrity level to decide whether a UAC bypass is worth attempting."
  }
